On Thu, 17 Jul 2003, Roger Broadbent wrote:

> From my (limited) exposure, it also seems that XML is horribly verbose. If
> anyone *were* to use XML in a meanignful sense, I would expect the
> save/bones file to be so large that compression would be necessary. This
> would neatly sidestep the too-easily-human-readable issue.
>
> I suspect that the parsing and generation code and the memory it requires
> at runtime would bloat the binary somewhat too.

As I said elsewhere, we cannot reasonably expect an unpatched version to
cope with games of a patched version, execpt for possible differences in
the flags structure indicating states of options added by a patch. So
what we need is compatibility in one direction only. For this, instead of
defining a totally compatible save file format, why not let patch writers
themself take care of it?

You add the monster with number n? If you load a saved game created by an
unpatched version, shift all monster indices i where i>=n upwards by 1.
You create the instrinsic "stunning resistance"? If you load an unpatched
saved game, recalculate the hero's attributes after loading and set his
intrinsic accordingly. (If he ate a monster conveying this intrinsic in
the unpatched version, bad luck.)

As the last example shows, this approach could yield to unsatisfying
results for saved games, but it is viable for bones.


